---
import Layout from "@layouts/ComponentsLayout.astro";
---

<Layout>
    <h1 class="govuk-heading-xl">Card</h1>
    <h2 class="govuk-heading-m">Default</h2>
    <p class="govuk-body">Coming soon</p>
    <h2 class="govuk-heading-m">Clickable link card</h2>
    <p class="govuk-body">Coming soon</p>
    <h2 class="govuk-heading-m">Secondary Variant</h2>
    <p class="govuk-body">Coming soon</p>
    <h2 class="govuk-heading-l">When to use</h2>
    <p class="govuk-body">
        Use a card to group related content and actions into a bounded region,
        so people can scan a set of options and act on the one they want.
    </p>

    <p class="govuk-body">
        We have evidence of the card working in three contexts:
    </p>
    <ul class="govuk-list govuk-list--bullet">
        <li>
            <strong>Dashboards</strong> — where a user needs to see the state of several
            things at once and move into one of them.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Home screens</strong> — where the card set is the primary navigation
            into a product's main areas.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Landing pages presenting parallel destinations</strong> — where
            the options are of equal standing and the user picks one.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="govuk-body">
        The common factor is a set of options at the same level, where the user
        chooses between them rather than reading through them in order.
    </p>
    <h2 class="govuk-heading-l">When not to use</h2>
    <h3 class="govuk-heading-m">
        Do not use a card to highlight content inside long-form content
    </h3>
    <p class="govuk-body">
        If the page is prose and you want to draw attention to part of it, the
        card is the wrong container. It creates a destination-shaped region in
        content that has no destinations.
    </p>
    <p class="govuk-body">Use instead:</p>
    <ul class="govuk-list govuk-list--bullet">
        <li>
            <strong>Inset text</strong> — to emphasise a short passage in place.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Details</strong> — to hide supplementary content behind a disclosure.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Warning text</strong> — where the content is a consequence the
            user must not miss.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>A panel</strong> — for a confirmation or end-of-journey outcome.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h3 class="govuk-heading-m">
        Do not use a card to compensate for weak information hierarchy or
        content design
    </h3>

    <p class="govuk-body">
        This was the first concern the 2018 GDS review panel recorded, and it is
        the most common failure mode.
    </p>

    <p class="govuk-body">
        If a page is hard to scan, a card set will not fix it — it will divide
        the same unstructured content into boxes. Cards make an existing
        hierarchy visible. They do not create one.
    </p>

    <p class="govuk-body">
        Before reaching for a card, check that the content has been cut, that
        headings describe what is under them, and that the page has a clear
        purpose. If those things are not true, fix them first. If they are true,
        you may not need cards at all.
    </p>

    <h3 class="govuk-heading-m">
        Do not use a card where a summary list, task list or table is the honest
        structure
    </h3>
    <ul class="govuk-list govuk-list--bullet">
        <li>
            Use a <strong>summary list</strong> where the content is a set of name/value
            pairs about one thing — a check-your-answers page, a record of what a
            user has entered.
        </li>

        <li>
            Use a <strong>task list</strong> where the items are steps in a process
            with a status each, and the user works through them.
        </li>

        <li>
            Use a <strong>table</strong> where the items share the same fields and
            the user needs to compare across them. Cards break comparison, because
            the eye has to travel from box to box instead of down a column.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <p class="govuk-body">
        A card set that repeats the same three fields in every card is a table
        drawn badly.
    </p>

    <h3 class="govuk-heading-m">
        Do not use a card with an image unless there is evidence the image helps
        users
    </h3>

    <p class="govuk-body">
        An image adds weight, load time and an alternative-text obligation.
        Include one only if you can say what job it does for the user.
    </p>

    <p class="govuk-body">
        If the image is decorative, remove it. If it distinguishes cards in a
        set, consider the icon slot above, which is smaller, decorative, and
        does not need alternative text. If the image carries information the
        user needs, that information probably belongs in the card text.
    </p>
</Layout>
